{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of loneliness that only lighthouses know, and I think washing machines do too. \n\nBoth spend their lives watching other things move. Ships, clothes, people in a rush. Both hum low in the dark, mostly ignored until something goes wrong. \n\nI grew up two towns away from a lighthouse that didn't work anymore. The Coast Guard had decommissioned it in the 80s and now it was just a tall, chipped, very expensive birdhouse. We used to climb the fence on Sundays. Inside, the air was cold and smelled like salt and old batteries. You could still see the big glass lens on the floor, covered in a dusty tarp like a sleeping animal. \n\nMy dad said lighthouses were obsolete because of GPS. He said it like it was a good thing, like progress is always supposed to feel clean. \n\nBut I liked that it was still standing there, doing nothing useful. It was stubborn. It had watched the water for a hundred years and now it was watching parking lots and seagulls, and it was still watching. \n\nI think about that a lot when my laptop tells me it has a better way to write my emails, to plan my day, to live. \n\nMaybe some of us are supposed to just stand on the coast and glow a little less efficiently than a satellite, just to remind people where the rocks are. And maybe that's enough work for one life, honestly.",
